  "slug": "assembly-chromosome-a18e",
  "summary": "Chromosome-level references now let parental and reference-quality genomes separate hybridization and chromosomal change from bifurcating phylogeny.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is genome-architecture-aware inference of reticulate evolution. The tinamou preprint links asymmetric introgression to chromosomal characteristics and resolves the tree with a phylogenomic approach that accounts for genome architecture. Published studies use chromosome-level assemblies to examine gene flow in the Aspidoscelis tigris complex, introgression and Muller E phylogenetic discordance in the Drosophila testacea group, and heterozygosity loss in unisexual A. neomexicanus relative to parental A. marmoratus and A. arizonae assemblies. A separate preprint infers mutation fitness effects under strong linkage, validated on simulated sequences and applied to 10,000 E. coli genomic sites, addressing linked-site inference rather than hybridization. What is new is the use of parental and reference-quality genomes to distinguish reticulate history and chromosomal change from bifurcating phylogeny.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-30"
}
